  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM EDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\n- Light Rain Tomorrow\n\nModels and ensembles are in great concurrence on the passage of \nan upper level trough with a corresponding mid level wave and cold\nfront. The initial trough will begin moving across the Lake late \ntonight, early tomorrow. Showers are possible beginning towards \nsunrise in the northwest. NAEFS ensembles has best PWATS in our \nregion in the Ludington region though the best moisture along the \nfront overall remains through northern lower. \n\nAs the front sweeps across the state throughout the day it will \nreinvigorate as it reaches southern lower, with showers possible \nalong and south of the I 94 corridor in the afternoon and early \nevening.   \n\nTemps tomorrow will remain fairly warm with highs in the upper 60s \nto low 70s.  After Max temps of 15 to 20 degrees above normal today \nand tomorrow, high's on Friday will seem pedestrian. However, highs \non Friday, in the low 69s, will still be around 5 to 10 degrees \nabove normal. Lows Thursday night into Friday will be closer to \nnormal, with lows in the 30s. \n\n- Scattered Showers Possible Saturday\n\n  Eyes continue to be on the October 31st forecast. The analysis \ncontinues to trend drier, though there is a persistent wave moving \nfrom the southwest through the daytime Saturday. Wave of mid level \nmoisture will trek through lower Michigan moving from the southwest \nto the northeast. The main question remains how much rain will \noccur. Trends in ensembles and mid range models have best chance\nof rain through the southwest with scattered showers moving across\nthe state through the afternoon. QPF overall is fairly sparse so\nlight rain is expected as this point. \n\n- Pattern shift with rain chances next week\n\n Sunday continues to show better moisture streaming into the Ohio\nValley and through southern lower Michigan late Sunday. This \ncould bring some showers chances along and south of I 96.\n\n There is an increasing disparity in the analysis onward. However, \nthe overall trend is for temps to warm back up above normal, 10 to\n15 degrees, by the middle of next week.\n Long range models are showing a system that should make its way\nthrough the inter mountain west and through the Great Plains next\nweek. Moisture boundaries moving off of this system could bring \nsome chances for showers, especially mid next week.",
